<body>
  <header class="site-header">
    <p class="brand">Null Pointers<span class="tagline">Five humans. One suspiciously optimistic build.</span></p>
    <div class="header-actions">
      <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
        <a href="#team">The team</a>
        <a href="#future-of-se">The future</a>
      </nav>
      <button id="theme-toggle" type="button" aria-label="Switch to night mode" aria-pressed="false">
        <span aria-hidden="true">☾</span> Day mode
      </button>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="page-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">A small team with big compile-time feelings</p>
      <h1 id="page-title">Meet the people behind the <span style="color:var(--accent)">pointers.</span></h1>
      <p class="hero-copy">We’re five computer science students building things, asking better questions, and occasionally staring at a missing curly brace like it owes us money.</p>
      <span class="hero-note">Current team status: works on our machines.</span>
    </section>

    <section id="team" aria-labelledby="team-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <h2 id="team-title">The team</h2>
        <p>Different interests, shared enthusiasm for good systems and very specific debugging rituals.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="team-grid">
        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:Mohammad.png}}" alt="Mohammad, a member of Null Pointers" loading="lazy">
          <h3>Mohammad</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science · 1st semester</p>
          <p class="bio">Mohammad studies computer science and is into full-stack development and gaming. He can patiently spend 40 hours with a story-driven game and skip nothing; a 30-second test pipeline, however, is apparently a very different kind of endurance sport. Today, he sees AI as a fast helper for boilerplate and syntax, but one that can miss subtle logic flaws and needs careful code review.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI doesn’t make bad developers good; it just helps them generate catastrophic technical debt at record speed.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:Nithya.png}}" alt="Nithya, a member of Null Pointers" loading="lazy">
          <h3>Nithya</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science · 7th semester, thesis stage</p>
          <p class="bio">Nithya is a computer science thesis-stage student interested in distributed systems, cloud architecture, badminton, and board games. She has rewritten her thesis abstract more times than she has recompiled her code—a productivity metric she did not ask to establish. In her view, AI speeds up familiar work but can be confidently wrong about complex legacy systems and concurrency, so a senior engineer still needs to steer.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI autocomplete hasn’t eliminated bugs; it has just made them compile much faster.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:Rafee.png}}" alt="Rafee, a member of Null Pointers" loading="lazy">
          <h3>Rafee</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science · 1st semester</p>
          <p class="bio">Rafee studies computer science and enjoys frontend UI/UX, the React and Next.js ecosystem, table tennis, and photography. He judges coffee shops by Wi-Fi stability and latency to GitHub, which is either excellent research or a cry for help. He finds AI a remarkable interactive tutor for confusing compiler errors, but warns that trusting generated output too early can feel like understanding without the underlying skills.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 If you can’t debug code without an LLM, you are the copilot, not the agent.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:Raghvendra.png}}" alt="Raghvendra, a member of Null Pointers" loading="lazy">
          <h3>Raghvendra</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science · 3rd semester</p>
          <p class="bio">Raghvendra studies computer science, with interests in machine learning infrastructure, data engineering, hiking, and chess. He will spend four hours automating a five-minute task and call it an investment—which, to be fair, is a very engineer-shaped sentence. He sees AI as useful for prototyping and translating business logic into boilerplate, but not as a substitute for context-aware architectural decisions.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 90% of current “AI agent” workflows are just fragile while loops wrapped around regex parsers.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:Sayemah.png}}" alt="Sayemah, a member of Null Pointers" loading="lazy">
          <h3>Sayemah</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science · 1st semester</p>
          <p class="bio">Sayemah is a computer science student interested in calligraphy, culinary arts, travel, and human-computer interaction. She can master a complex traditional recipe on the first try, but one unmatched curly brace can still ruin the evening. She sees AI as a creative prototyping tool that lowers the barrier to new ideas, while reminding us that functional code still needs human communication, interface empathy, and aesthetic intention.</p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 Prompt engineering isn’t the future of software engineering; thoughtful human design is.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="future-of-se" class="future-section" aria-labelledby="future-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <h2 id="future-title">The Future of Software Engineering</h2>
        <p>Our shared forecast: less time typing the obvious, more time making sure the clever stuff is actually right.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="future-grid">
        <article class="future-card">
          <p class="label">Right now · copilot, not autopilot</p>
          <h3>AI is fast. Context is still the hard part.</h3>
          <p>Across the team, AI is a useful partner for boilerplate, prototypes, syntax lookups, and getting unstuck. It can also invent confidence, miss edge cases, and make a bad assumption compile beautifully. We treat its output as a draft: review it, test it, and ask what it does when the network flakes out at 2 a.m.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="future-card">
          <p class="label">In 5–10 years · humans set the constraints</p>
          <h3>Engineering becomes more about intent.</h3>
          <p>We expect more agent-built and integrated software, guided by people who define requirements, architecture, failure modes, privacy boundaries, and what “good” means for real users. The valuable work shifts toward verification, resilient systems, accessibility, and thoughtful design—not simply producing more code per minute.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="closing-line">Our forecast is optimistic. Our code review checklist remains less trusting.</p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer">
    <p>Made with ❤ and AI. 2026 · Null Pointers</p>
  </footer>

  <script>
    (() => {
      const root = document.documentElement;
      const toggle = document.getElementById("theme-toggle");

      function setTheme(theme) {
        const isNight = theme === "night";
        root.dataset.theme = isNight ? "night" : "day";
        toggle.setAttribute("aria-label", isNight ? "Switch to day mode" : "Switch to night mode");
        toggle.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(isNight));
        toggle.innerHTML = isNight
          ? '<span aria-hidden="true">☀</span> Night mode'
          : '<span aria-hidden="true">☾</span> Day mode';
        document.querySelector('meta[name="theme-color"]').content = isNight ? "#14142B" : "#FFF8E7";
      }

      toggle.addEventListener("click", () => {
        setTheme(root.dataset.theme === "night" ? "day" : "night");
      });
    })();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
